@ This routine is a single argument function which provides the integrand
in a calculation of velocity-weighted averages of a user-specified
function over a user-specified distribution function. The principle
use of this routine is to compute a velocity-weighted reaction rate 
\begin{equation}
\label{ereactionrate}
\langle \sigma v v^{|n|}\rangle = \frac{4 \pi}{\alpha m} \left( \frac{2}{m} 
\right)^{(n+2)/2} \int_{0}^{\infty} dE \: \sigma f(E) E^{(n+2)/2},
\end{equation} 
where $\sigma(E)$ is computed
using the function |sigma|, $f(E)$ is calculated by 
the function |edist|, $m$ is the
particle mass relating $E$ and $v$ via $E = \frac{1}{2} m v^{2}$, and $\alpha$
is the normalization constant for the distribution function,
\[ 
\alpha = \frac{4 \pi}{m} \left( \frac{2}{m} 
\right)^{1/2} \int_{0}^{\infty} dE \: f(E) E^{1/2}.
\] 
Note that $\alpha$ does not appear in this routine but is incorporated
in the calling routines. 

A number of assumptions must be made in arriving
at Eq.~(\ref{ereactionrate}).
\begin{enumerate}
  \item $\sigma$ is a function only of the magnitude of the relative
velocities of the colliding particles.
  \item The distribution function is a function only of the particle's
energy (or, equivalently, speed).
  \item The velocity of the ``projectile'' particle is much less than
the typical velocity of the ``target'' particles (i.e., defined by $f$).
\end{enumerate}
These are the standard assumptions made when a neutral atom (``projectile'')
collides with plasma electrons (``target'').

This routine is set up with a single argument so that it can be used with
``canned'' integration routines; the argument |pe| is the energy $E$ in 
the above expressions. The mass $m =$ |xmass| and exponent |n| must come in 
through a common block.

@<Functions...@>=
      real function electron_rate_integrand(pe)
      implicit_none

      integer n                  // Input
      real pe,xmass
      common /com_erint/ n,xmass

      real zconst,zedist,zexp,zint,zsigma  // Local variables
      real edist,sigma          // External functions

      zsigma=sigma(pe)
      zedist=edist(pe)
      zexp=half*(areal(n)+two)
      zconst=(const(4.0) / xmass) * (two / xmass)**zexp

      zint=zconst * zsigma * zedist * pe**zexp

      electron_rate_integrand=zint

      return
      end

@ This routine is a single argument function which provides the integrand
in a calculation of a standard integral closely associated with 
velocity-weighted reaction rates. 
More specifically, these integrals arise in the
computation of plasma source terms due to interactions between neutral
atoms and charged particles\cite{Reit93}, $S_{A_{p},k}$,
\begin{equation}
\label{source}
S_{A_{p},k} = \Gamma \int d^{3}v \: f(\vec{v}) \langle \sigma_{k}(v_{rel})
\Delta A_{p} \rangle_{p},
\end{equation}
where $\Gamma$ is the flux of neutral particles, $f(\vec{v})$ is the neutral
particle distribution function, $p$ is a plasma species index, $\sigma_{k}$ is 
the cross section for the $k$th reaction between the neutral and species
$p$, $v_{rel}$ is the relative velocity of the two particles, and 
$\Delta A_{p}$ is the change in a plasma quantity (e.g., density, momentum,
energy, etc.) in a single collision. 

When the plasma species can be
described by a Maxwellian distribution function, the required cross
section averages in Eq.(\ref{source}) can be computed in terms of the standard 
integral\cite{Reit93}
\begin{equation}
\label{iln}
I_{\ell, |n|} \equiv \frac{ \exp( -u^{2} / u_{p}^{2}) }
                     {\sqrt{\pi} u u_{p}^{n+1}}
                     \int_{0}^{\infty} dv_{rel} \: v_{rel}^{2+|n|}
                     \sigma_{\ell}(v_{rel}) e^{-v^{2}_{rel} / u_{p}^{2}}
                     \left[ \exp(2 v_{rel} u / u_{p}^{2}) 
                            - (-1)^{n}\exp(-2 v_{rel} u / u_{p}^{2})\right],
\end{equation}
where $u$ is the velocity of the neutral particle, and $u_{p}$ is the
thermal velocity of the plasma particle. The latter is related to the
plasma temperature by $T_{p} = \frac{1}{2} m_{p} u_{p}^{2}$. The subscript
$\ell$ is included to allow the treatment of elastic collisions
($\ell \neq 0$). Note that Eq.~(\ref{iln}) is not the same as 
$\langle \sigma v v^{n} \rangle$ because of the need to treat angular
dependence in the integrand of Eq.~(\ref{source}), e.g., for
the momentum source rate. Quantities such as this can be found as a linear
combination of the $I_{\ell, |n|}$. However, the result provided by
this routine differs from the definition of Eq.~(\ref{iln}) by a factor
of $u_{p}^{n}$ so as to have the same dimensions as 
$\langle \sigma v v^{n} \rangle$.

This routine is set up with a single argument so that it can be used with
``canned'' integration routines; the argument |pv| is the relative
velocity $v_{rel}$ in the above expressions. The factors $u$ and $u_{p}$,
as well as the exponent |n| come in through a common block. Also in 
common is the mass |xmass| used to convert the relative velocity into
an energy for use in evaluating the cross section |sigma|. The value
to be used for |xmass| may vary, depending on the origin of the data used
in |sigma|.

@<Functions...@>=
      real function ion_rate_integrand(pv)
      implicit_none

      integer n                  // Input
      real pv,u,up,xmass
      common /com_irint/ n,u,up,xmass

      real zbracket,zconst,ze,zexparg,zint,zsigma  // Local 
      real sigma                // External function

      ze=half*xmass*pv**2
      zsigma=sigma(ze)
      zexparg=two*pv*u/up**2
      zbracket=exp(zexparg)-(-1)**n*exp(-zexparg)
      zconst=exp(-(u/up)**2)/(sqrt(pi)*u*up)

      zint=zconst*pv**(2+n)*zsigma*exp(-(pv/up)**2)*zbracket

      ion_rate_integrand=zint

      return
      end

@ This routine provides the electron distribution function at the
energy |pe|. Presently, the distribution is assumed to be Maxwellian;
the temperature |temp| characterizing the distribution is passed in through
common. Note that |temp| and |pe| must have the same units.
The routine utilizes a single argument for generality since 
other distributions may require additional parameters (beyond temperature)
to describe them.  (?? Could we do it with a macro?)

@<Functions...@>=
      real function edist(pe)   // Use a longer name?
      implicit_none

      real pe, temp            // Input
      common /com_edist/ temp

      edist = exp(-pe/temp)

      return
      end

@ This is a prototype routine which serves as the intermediary between the
integration functions and calls to ALADDIN subroutines. In order to fit
in with the former, this function must have only a single argument;
consequently, it could easily be altered without having to change the
calling routines. 

As presently used, this routine computes the cross section at the
input energy |pe|. The other information required to communicate with
ALADDIN is brought in by common blocks (?? Another instance where
we could use a macro?).

@<Functions...@>=
      real function sigma(pe)
      implicit_none

/* Parameters: same as in ALCOM.FOR */
      integer ncfmax
      parameter (ncfmax=200)

      character*40 leval                  // Input
      integer ncf
      real pe
      double precision cf(ncfmx)
      common /com_sigala/ leval,ncf,cf

      double precision ze,zfit               // Local
      character*80 errmsg

      ze=dble(pe)         // Explicitly transfer to double precision

      call aladdin_eval(leval,ze,cf,ncf,zfit,errmsg)

/* Trap errors from ALADDIN routines. Barring actual foul-ups, the
   the only likely error is from requesting cross section values at
   energies below threshold. A handful of the ALADDIN routines report
   this as an error condition. Assume this is the case here, but
   write out the error message as a warning.                          */
      if (errmsg != ' ') then
        zfit=0.0d0
        write(stderr,*)' Warning from sigma & aladdin_eval: ',errmsg
      end if

      sigma=areal(zfit)   // Explicitly convert back to normal precision

      return
      end



